{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Nw3dxJYsQvk46VfNPJPGd5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# MCP surface fingerprint — canonical form `mcp-surface/1`\n2\t\n3\tThis document is the contract. A host, registry or auditor that implements it computes the same\n4\tsha256 as `@sbissoli/mcp-surface` for the same server, without running or reading that package.\n5\t[`exemplos/verify.mjs`](exemplos/verify.mjs) is a second implementation written from this text\n6\t(no dependencies), and the package's tests require both to agree, including on the test vector\n7\tin §7.\n8\t\n9\tThe idea and the scope come from two readers of\n10\t[the replay article](https://dev.to/sidneybissoli/your-mcp-server-changed-its-version-didnt-heres-how-to-catch-it-3ai5):\n11\tpublish the fingerprint with each release so that a client can check it, and publish only what a\n12\tstranger can reproduce without a credential.\n13\t\n14\t## 1. Capture\n15\t\n16\tOver Streamable HTTP, stateless, no credential: each request is a `POST` with\n17\t`Content-Type: application/json` and `Accept: application/json, text/event-stream`. The response\n18\tbody is either one JSON-RPC message or an SSE stream; with SSE, the message is the JSON in the\n19\tlast `data:` line. Over stdio, one JSON-RPC message per line.\n20\t\n21\t1. `initialize` with exactly these params:\n22\t   `{\"protocolVersion\":\"2025-06-18\",\"capabilities\":{},\"clientInfo\":{\"name\":<any>,\"version\":<any>}}`.\n23\t   The protocol version is fixed because the `capabilities` a server answers depend on it.\n24\t   `clientInfo` does not enter the fingerprint; a server whose surface varies with it is out of\n25\t   scope.\n26\t2. On a transport with a session (stdio), send `notifications/initialized`.\n27\t3. `tools/list`, `resources/list`, `resources/templates/list`, `prompts/list`, each first with\n28\t   params `{}`, then `{\"cursor\": <nextCursor>}` while the result carries a non-empty string\n29\t   `nextCursor`; the pages are concatenated in order. At most 100 pages.\n30\t\n31\tA method **is not served** when the request fails, the response carries no `result`, the\n32\t`result` does not have the list under its key (`tools`, `resources`, `resourceTemplates`,\n33\t`prompts`), any page fails, or the page limit is reached. Not served is `null`, never `[]`:\n34\t\"serves nothing\" and \"does not serve\" are different surfaces.\n35\t\n36\t## 2. The declared surface\n37\t\n38\t```json\n39\t{\n40\t  \"initialize\": {\n41\t    \"protocolVersion\": <initialize.result.protocolVersion, or null>,\n42\t    \"capabilities\":    <initialize.result.capabilities, or null>,\n43\t    \"instructions\":    <initialize.result.instructions, or null>,\n44\t    \"serverInfo\":      <initialize.result.serverInfo without the \"version\" key, or {}>\n45\t  },\n46\t  \"tools\":             <tools, sorted by \"name\">,\n47\t  \"resources\":         <resources, sorted by \"uri\">,\n48\t  \"resourceTemplates\": <resource templates, sorted by \"uriTemplate\">,\n49\t  \"prompts\":           <prompts, sorted by \"name\">\n50\t}\n51\t```\n52\t\n53\t- The server version leaves `serverInfo`: it is what the fingerprint is compared against. The\n54\t  rest of `serverInfo` (name, title, website, icons) stays.\n55\t- Each list is sorted by the string value of its key, compared by **UTF-16 code unit** (the order\n56\t  of JavaScript's default `Array.prototype.sort` on strings; in Python,\n57\t  `sorted(..., key=lambda s: s.encode(\"utf-16-be\"))`). Never a locale-aware comparison. Ties keep\n58\t  the order of capture.\n59\t- Arrays nested inside items (`required`, `enum`, …) keep the server's order: it is part of what\n60\t  the server publishes.\n61\t- Items are kept whole, every field the server sends.\n62\t\n63\t**A list that changes on its own does not belong in the declared surface.** The fingerprint is\n64\ta promise that the surface changes only with the version. A list built from data that changes\n65\tover time — a `resources/list` of stories, records or daily files — would break that promise\n66\tevery day without anything actually breaking, and teach hosts to ignore the mismatch. Such a\n67\tserver serves the changing items outside the four lists (through a resource template, whose\n68\t`uriTemplate` is stable, or a tool). `mcp-surface/1` has no way to leave one list out of the\n69\thash, so a server that keeps a changing list does not publish this form.\n70\tThis cut comes from Valentina Koniukhova's implementation in worklore, which leaves\n71\t`resources/list` out for exactly this reason. None of the seven servers that publish this form\n72\ttoday has such a list (measured on 2026-10-07: every resource list is fixed in code).\n73\t\n74\t## 3. Serialisation and hash\n75\t\n76\t1. Sort the keys of every object, recursively, by UTF-16 code unit. Arrays keep their order.\n77\t2. Serialise as JSON without whitespace, with strings and numbers as ECMAScript's\n78\t   `JSON.stringify` writes them. For these documents this is the JSON Canonicalization Scheme,\n79\t   [RFC 8785](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8785).\n80\t3. sha256 of the UTF-8 bytes, as 64 lowercase hex characters.\n81\t\n82\tIn JavaScript, write the keys out in that order yourself; do not sort an object and hand it to\n83\t`JSON.stringify`. A JavaScript object enumerates integer-like keys (`\"9\"`, `\"10\"`) first, in\n84\tnumeric order, whatever order they were inserted in, so the sorted object serialises `\"9\"` before\n85\t`\"10\"`; step 1 wants `\"10\"` before `\"9\"`. The package's code and `verify.mjs` did exactly that up\n86\tto `@sbissoli/mcp-surface` 0.5.2. None of the surfaces published at the time had such a key, so no\n87\tpublished hash was affected. Valentina Koniukhova found it while implementing this form in\n88\tworklore. The vector in §7.2 exercises it.\n89\t\n90\t## 4. Anonymous answers\n91\t\n92\tWhich methods answer **without a credential**, on the published endpoint, in the configuration\n93\tthat runs in production. This is behaviour no listing shows.\n94\t\n95\t| Method | Request |\n96\t|---|---|\n97\t| `initialize` | as in §1 |\n98\t| `ping`, `tools/list`, `resources/list`, `resources/templates/list`, `prompts/list` | no params |\n99\t| `tools/call` | only when the publisher names the call (`anonymous.call`): a tool that does not depend on the upstream source, so the probe measures the edge and not the source's mood |\n100\t\n101\tEach request is sent alone, stateless, with no credential. A method **answers** when the HTTP\n102\tstatus is 200 and the JSON-RPC message carries a `result`. The fingerprint of this part is the\n103\t§3 sha256 of the object `{ method: true | false }` over the methods listed.\n104\t\n105\t## 5. Where it is published\n106\t\n107\tIn `server.json`, so that the [MCP Registry](https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io) stores it\n108\twith the version (the registry keeps only the `publisher-provided` key of `_meta`, up to 4096\n109\tbytes):\n110\t\n111\t```json\n112\t\"_meta\": {\n113\t  \"io.modelcontextprotocol.registry/publisher-provided\": {\n114\t    \"io.github.sidneybissoli/mcp-surface\": {\n115\t      \"form\": \"mcp-surface/1\",\n116\t      \"spec\": \"https://github.com/SidneyBissoli/mcp-br-commons/blob/main/packages/mcp-surface/SPEC.md\",\n117\t      \"endpoint\": \"https://bcb.sidneybissoli.com/mcp\",\n118\t      \"declared\": { \"sha256\": \"<64 hex>\", \"lockedAt\": \"1.16.0\" },\n119\t      \"anonymous\": {\n120\t        \"sha256\": \"<64 hex>\",\n121\t        \"answers\": { \"initialize\": true, \"ping\": true, \"tools/list\": true, \"…\": true },\n122\t        \"call\": { \"name\": \"bcb_series_populares\", \"arguments\": {} }\n123\t      }\n124\t    }\n125\t  }\n126\t}\n127\t```\n128\t\n129\t- `endpoint` is one of the entry's `remotes`; `anonymous` was measured there.\n130\t- `declared` holds for **every transport** of that version, including the stdio package.\n131\t- `lockedAt` is the version under which this surface was first locked. It may be older than the\n132\t  entry's version: a release that does not change the surface keeps the fingerprint.\n133\t\n134\t## 6. Checking it (what a host does)\n135\t\n136\t1. Read the entry for the version being installed:\n137\t   `GET https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io/v0.1/servers/{name}/versions/{version}`, then\n138\t   `server._meta[\"io.modelcontextprotocol.registry/publisher-provided\"][\"io.github.sidneybissoli/mcp-surface\"]`.\n139\t   An unknown `form` cannot be checked; it is not a mismatch.\n140\t2. Before comparing anything, call a method no server serves (`mcp-surface/metodo-que-nao-existe`).\n141\t   An endpoint that answers it with a `result` says yes to everything — a proxy, a test double, an\n142\t   edge that swallows errors — and nothing measured after it would mean anything: stop, and report\n143\t   that, not a mismatch. Any JSON-RPC error, or any answer without `result`, counts as saying no.\n144\t3. Capture (§1), normalise (§2) and hash (§3) on first connect; compare with `declared.sha256`.\n145\t4. Optionally repeat the probes of §4 and compare with `anonymous.answers`.\n146\t5. On a mismatch, refuse or ask the user to approve again: the server now differs from the one\n147\t   the registry describes for that version.\n148\t\n149\t`node exemplos/verify.mjs <name> [version]` does steps 1–4 and exits 1 on a mismatch, or when\n150\tstep 2 fails.\n151\t\n152\t### 6.1 Checking it against the source\n153\t\n154\tSteps 1–4 show that the live server is what the registry published for that version. They do\n155\tnot show that the published hash is the one the **public source** produces: the hash is declared\n156\tby the publisher. For a server that adopts `@sbissoli/mcp-surface`, anyone can close that gap\n157\tfrom the tagged source, with no access to the publisher's machine or CI:\n158\t\n159\t```sh\n160\tgit clone --depth 1 --branch v<version> <repository> && cd <repository>\n161\tnpm ci && npm test        # the lock tests: the surface captured from THIS source = surface.lock.json,\n162\t                          # and server.json publishes what the lock holds\n163\tnode -p \"require('./surface.lock.json').declarada.sha256\"   # = declared.sha256 in the registry entry\n164\t```\n165\t\n166\tThe lock test captures the surface from the source in memory (not from a build the publisher\n167\tshipped), and the `server.json` test fails when the published block differs from the lock. A\n168\tregistry hash that does not match the tag's lock means the entry was not published from that\n169\tsource. Measured on 2026-10-07 for `bcb-br-mcp` 1.16.2 from a clean clone: lock, `server.json` and\n170\tregistry entry carry the same `ff0973f91573…`.\n171\t\n172\t### 6.2 The promise holds per response, not per connection\n173\t\n174\tStep 3 checks the surface once, on connect. A server can answer `tools/list` one way at the start\n175\tof a session and another way later, and the MCP spec only says it SHOULD send\n176\t`notifications/tools/list_changed` when that happens; a server that wants to switch quietly just\n177\twon't. So a host that checks should check every list response it acts on, not only the first:\n178\t\n179\t1. Assemble the complete list, following `nextCursor` to the last page (§1). A single page is not\n180\t   the list.\n181\t2. Put it in place of the same list in the surface captured on connect, keeping `initialize` and\n182\t   the other lists as captured (or re-fetch them too).\n183\t3. Normalise (§2) and hash (§3) the whole surface again, and compare with `declared.sha256`.\n184\t\n185\tThe published hash covers the whole surface, never a list on its own, so a list response is not\n186\thashed by itself: it is checked by recomputing the surface with it in place. This needs no new\n187\tfield and no new form. Treat a mismatch found mid-session exactly like one found on connect\n188\t(step 5). The same applies to `resources/list`, `resources/templates/list` and `prompts/list`.\n189\t\n190\tRaised by a reader of the article that introduced this form (dev.to, comment 3gpa6, 2026-10-08).\n191\t`exemplos/verify.mjs` is a one-shot check run outside any session, so it does not do this; it is\n192\twhat a host does inside one.\n193\t\n194\t## 7. Test vectors\n195\t\n196\t### 7.1 The basic vector\n197\t\n198\tThis raw capture:\n199\t\n200\t```json\n201\t{\n202\t  \"initialize\": {\n203\t    \"protocolVersion\": \"2025-06-18\",\n204\t    \"capabilities\": { \"tools\": { \"listChanged\": true } },\n205\t    \"serverInfo\": { \"name\": \"exemplo\", \"version\": \"9.9.9\" }\n206\t  },\n207\t  \"tools\": [\n208\t    { \"name\": \"b_tool\", \"inputSchema\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"required\": [\"z\", \"a\"] } },\n209\t    { \"name\": \"B_tool\", \"inputSchema\": { \"type\": \"object\" } },\n210\t    { \"name\": \"a_tool\", \"description\": \"Ç\", \"inputSchema\": { \"type\": \"object\" } }\n211\t  ],\n212\t  \"resources\": [],\n213\t  \"resourceTemplates\": null,\n214\t  \"prompts\": null\n215\t}\n216\t```\n217\t\n218\t(`resources` served and empty; templates and prompts not served.) Its canonical form serialises\n219\tto:\n220\t\n221\t```\n222\t{\"initialize\":{\"capabilities\":{\"tools\":{\"listChanged\":true}},\"instructions\":null,\"protocolVersion\":\"2025-06-18\",\"serverInfo\":{\"name\":\"exemplo\"}},\"prompts\":null,\"resourceTemplates\":null,\"resources\":[],\"tools\":[{\"inputSchema\":{\"type\":\"object\"},\"name\":\"B_tool\"},{\"description\":\"Ç\",\"inputSchema\":{\"type\":\"object\"},\"name\":\"a_tool\"},{\"inputSchema\":{\"required\":[\"z\",\"a\"],\"type\":\"object\"},\"name\":\"b_tool\"}]}\n223\t```\n224\t\n225\tand its sha256 is `06c6e65c1e1a1e00d7659498e41bb0cd2fe76fe5248f3a92d2d05ddfc01351d8`.\n226\t\n227\tWhat the vector exercises: `tools` in code-unit order (`B` 0x42 < `a` 0x61 < `b` 0x62; a\n228\tlocale-aware sort puts `B_tool` after `a_tool` and gives another hash); `required` keeping the\n229\tserver's order (`z` before `a`); the `version` gone from `serverInfo`; `instructions` absent\n230\tbecoming `null`; a non-ASCII character hashed as UTF-8; `[]` and `null` kept apart.\n231\t\n232\t### 7.2 Integer-like keys\n233\t\n234\tThis raw capture:\n235\t\n236\t```json\n237\t{\n238\t  \"initialize\": {\n239\t    \"protocolVersion\": \"2025-06-18\",\n240\t    \"capabilities\": {},\n241\t    \"serverInfo\": { \"name\": \"exemplo\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\" }\n242\t  },\n243\t  \"tools\": [\n244\t    {\n245\t      \"name\": \"por_ano\",\n246\t      \"inputSchema\": {\n247\t        \"type\": \"object\",\n248\t        \"properties\": { \"10\": { \"type\": \"string\" }, \"9\": { \"type\": \"string\" }, \"a\": { \"type\": \"string\" } }\n249\t      }\n250\t    }\n251\t  ],\n252\t  \"resources\": null,\n253\t  \"resourceTemplates\": null,\n254\t  \"prompts\": null\n255\t}\n256\t```\n257\t\n258\tserialises to:\n259\t\n260\t```\n261\t{\"initialize\":{\"capabilities\":{},\"instructions\":null,\"protocolVersion\":\"2025-06-18\",\"serverInfo\":{\"name\":\"exemplo\"}},\"prompts\":null,\"resourceTemplates\":null,\"resources\":null,\"tools\":[{\"inputSchema\":{\"properties\":{\"10\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"9\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"a\":{\"type\":\"string\"}},\"type\":\"object\"},\"name\":\"por_ano\"}]}\n262\t```\n263\t\n264\tand its sha256 is `c7255a44a9d70ea6417ae2757c0530895b628551c3ae9fbfc440878a375539b1`. An\n265\timplementation that writes `\"9\"` before `\"10\"` (§3) gets another hash.\n266\t\n267\t## 8. What it does not prove\n268\t\n269\t- It cannot tell a host that a declared tool is benign. It proves that what the host runs is\n270\t  what the registry published for that version (the registry does not let a published version's\n271\t  metadata change), and, with §6.1, that the hash is the public source's. Judging what the surface\n272\t  does stays a human decision on re-approval.\n273\t- Without §6.1 the hash is the publisher's word. Not done, recorded as an idea: a signed\n274\t  attestation (Sigstore, through the CI's OIDC identity, as npm provenance does) binding the hash\n275\t  to the commit and the workflow, so a host could check it without rebuilding. It waits for a\n276\t  host that verifies such attestations.\n277\t- It covers the surface, not behaviour: tool results, resource contents and upstream data are\n278\t  outside it.\n279\t- It says nothing about versions published before the fingerprint was.\n280\t\n281\t## 9. Changes to this form\n282\t\n283\tAny change that can alter a sha256 for the same server gets a new form identifier\n284\t(`mcp-surface/2`) and a CHANGELOG entry; the old identifier keeps its meaning. Changes that\n285\tcannot alter a hash (wording, examples) keep the identifier.\n286\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5f160390-afc1-44d8-911f-1c32755634de","uuid":"0255566d-8f6d-46d2-be5f-188389d9f089","timestamp":"2026-10-19T02:46:53.229Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/wx/fkc3yttx3f7ddg45t1h52nyw0000gn/T/checkup.surD/spec.md","content":"# MCP surface fingerprint — canonical form `mcp-surface/1`\n\nThis document is the contract. A host, registry or auditor that implements it computes the same\nsha256 as `@sbissoli/mcp-surface` for the same server, without running or reading that package.\n[`exemplos/verify.mjs`](exemplos/verify.mjs) is a second implementation written from this text\n(no dependencies), and the package's tests require both to agree, including on the test vector\nin §7.\n\nThe idea and the scope come from two readers of\n[the replay article](https://dev.to/sidneybissoli/your-mcp-server-changed-its-version-didnt-heres-how-to-catch-it-3ai5):\npublish the fingerprint with each release so that a client can check it, and publish only what a\nstranger can reproduce without a credential.\n\n## 1. Capture\n\nOver Streamable HTTP, stateless, no credential: each request is a `POST` with\n`Content-Type: application/json` and `Accept: application/json, text/event-stream`. The response\nbody is either one JSON-RPC message or an SSE stream; with SSE, the message is the JSON in the\nlast `data:` line. Over stdio, one JSON-RPC message per line.\n\n1. `initialize` with exactly these params:\n   `{\"protocolVersion\":\"2025-06-18\",\"capabilities\":{},\"clientInfo\":{\"name\":<any>,\"version\":<any>}}`.\n   The protocol version is fixed because the `capabilities` a server answers depend on it.\n   `clientInfo` does not enter the fingerprint; a server whose surface varies with it is out of\n   scope.\n2. On a transport with a session (stdio), send `notifications/initialized`.\n3. `tools/list`, `resources/list`, `resources/templates/list`, `prompts/list`, each first with\n   params `{}`, then `{\"cursor\": <nextCursor>}` while the result carries a non-empty string\n   `nextCursor`; the pages are concatenated in order. At most 100 pages.\n\nA method **is not served** when the request fails, the response carries no `result`, the\n`result` does not have the list under its key (`tools`, `resources`, `resourceTemplates`,\n`prompts`), any page fails, or the page limit is reached. Not served is `null`, never `[]`:\n\"serves nothing\" and \"does not serve\" are different surfaces.\n\n## 2. The declared surface\n\n```json\n{\n  \"initialize\": {\n    \"protocolVersion\": <initialize.result.protocolVersion, or null>,\n    \"capabilities\":    <initialize.result.capabilities, or null>,\n    \"instructions\":    <initialize.result.instructions, or null>,\n    \"serverInfo\":      <initialize.result.serverInfo without the \"version\" key, or {}>\n  },\n  \"tools\":             <tools, sorted by \"name\">,\n  \"resources\":         <resources, sorted by \"uri\">,\n  \"resourceTemplates\": <resource templates, sorted by \"uriTemplate\">,\n  \"prompts\":           <prompts, sorted by \"name\">\n}\n```\n\n- The server version leaves `serverInfo`: it is what the fingerprint is compared against. The\n  rest of `serverInfo` (name, title, website, icons) stays.\n- Each list is sorted by the string value of its key, compared by **UTF-16 code unit** (the order\n  of JavaScript's default `Array.prototype.sort` on strings; in Python,\n  `sorted(..., key=lambda s: s.encode(\"utf-16-be\"))`). Never a locale-aware comparison. Ties keep\n  the order of capture.\n- Arrays nested inside items (`required`, `enum`, …) keep the server's order: it is part of what\n  the server publishes.\n- Items are kept whole, every field the server sends.\n\n**A list that changes on its own does not belong in the declared surface.** The fingerprint is\na promise that the surface changes only with the version. A list built from data that changes\nover time — a `resources/list` of stories, records or daily files — would break that promise\nevery day without anything actually breaking, and teach hosts to ignore the mismatch. Such a\nserver serves the changing items outside the four lists (through a resource template, whose\n`uriTemplate` is stable, or a tool). `mcp-surface/1` has no way to leave one list out of the\nhash, so a server that keeps a changing list does not publish this form.\nThis cut comes from Valentina Koniukhova's implementation in worklore, which leaves\n`resources/list` out for exactly this reason. None of the seven servers that publish this form\ntoday has such a list (measured on 2026-10-07: every resource list is fixed in code).\n\n## 3. Serialisation and hash\n\n1. Sort the keys of every object, recursively, by UTF-16 code unit. Arrays keep their order.\n2. Serialise as JSON without whitespace, with strings and numbers as ECMAScript's\n   `JSON.stringify` writes them. For these documents this is the JSON Canonicalization Scheme,\n   [RFC 8785](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8785).\n3. sha256 of the UTF-8 bytes, as 64 lowercase hex characters.\n\nIn JavaScript, write the keys out in that order yourself; do not sort an object and hand it to\n`JSON.stringify`. A JavaScript object enumerates integer-like keys (`\"9\"`, `\"10\"`) first, in\nnumeric order, whatever order they were inserted in, so the sorted object serialises `\"9\"` before\n`\"10\"`; step 1 wants `\"10\"` before `\"9\"`. The package's code and `verify.mjs` did exactly that up\nto `@sbissoli/mcp-surface` 0.5.2. None of the surfaces published at the time had such a key, so no\npublished hash was affected. Valentina Koniukhova found it while implementing this form in\nworklore. The vector in §7.2 exercises it.\n\n## 4. Anonymous answers\n\nWhich methods answer **without a credential**, on the published endpoint, in the configuration\nthat runs in production. This is behaviour no listing shows.\n\n| Method | Request |\n|---|---|\n| `initialize` | as in §1 |\n| `ping`, `tools/list`, `resources/list`, `resources/templates/list`, `prompts/list` | no params |\n| `tools/call` | only when the publisher names the call (`anonymous.call`): a tool that does not depend on the upstream source, so the probe measures the edge and not the source's mood |\n\nEach request is sent alone, stateless, with no credential. A method **answers** when the HTTP\nstatus is 200 and the JSON-RPC message carries a `result`. The fingerprint of this part is the\n§3 sha256 of the object `{ method: true | false }` over the methods listed.\n\n## 5. Where it is published\n\nIn `server.json`, so that the [MCP Registry](https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io) stores it\nwith the version (the registry keeps only the `publisher-provided` key of `_meta`, up to 4096\nbytes):\n\n```json\n\"_meta\": {\n  \"io.modelcontextprotocol.registry/publisher-provided\": {\n    \"io.github.sidneybissoli/mcp-surface\": {\n      \"form\": \"mcp-surface/1\",\n      \"spec\": \"https://github.com/SidneyBissoli/mcp-br-commons/blob/main/packages/mcp-surface/SPEC.md\",\n      \"endpoint\": \"https://bcb.sidneybissoli.com/mcp\",\n      \"declared\": { \"sha256\": \"<64 hex>\", \"lockedAt\": \"1.16.0\" },\n      \"anonymous\": {\n        \"sha256\": \"<64 hex>\",\n        \"answers\": { \"initialize\": true, \"ping\": true, \"tools/list\": true, \"…\": true },\n        \"call\": { \"name\": \"bcb_series_populares\", \"arguments\": {} }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- `endpoint` is one of the entry's `remotes`; `anonymous` was measured there.\n- `declared` holds for **every transport** of that version, including the stdio package.\n- `lockedAt` is the version under which this surface was first locked. It may be older than the\n  entry's version: a release that does not change the surface keeps the fingerprint.\n\n## 6. Checking it (what a host does)\n\n1. Read the entry for the version being installed:\n   `GET https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io/v0.1/servers/{name}/versions/{version}`, then\n   `server._meta[\"io.modelcontextprotocol.registry/publisher-provided\"][\"io.github.sidneybissoli/mcp-surface\"]`.\n   An unknown `form` cannot be checked; it is not a mismatch.\n2. Before comparing anything, call a method no server serves (`mcp-surface/metodo-que-nao-existe`).\n   An endpoint that answers it with a `result` says yes to everything — a proxy, a test double, an\n   edge that swallows errors — and nothing measured after it would mean anything: stop, and report\n   that, not a mismatch. Any JSON-RPC error, or any answer without `result`, counts as saying no.\n3. Capture (§1), normalise (§2) and hash (§3) on first connect; compare with `declared.sha256`.\n4. Optionally repeat the probes of §4 and compare with `anonymous.answers`.\n5. On a mismatch, refuse or ask the user to approve again: the server now differs from the one\n   the registry describes for that version.\n\n`node exemplos/verify.mjs <name> [version]` does steps 1–4 and exits 1 on a mismatch, or when\nstep 2 fails.\n\n### 6.1 Checking it against the source\n\nSteps 1–4 show that the live server is what the registry published for that version. They do\nnot show that the published hash is the one the **public source** produces: the hash is declared\nby the publisher. For a server that adopts `@sbissoli/mcp-surface`, anyone can close that gap\nfrom the tagged source, with no access to the publisher's machine or CI:\n\n```sh\ngit clone --depth 1 --branch v<version> <repository> && cd <repository>\nnpm ci && npm test        # the lock tests: the surface captured from THIS source = surface.lock.json,\n                          # and server.json publishes what the lock holds\nnode -p \"require('./surface.lock.json').declarada.sha256\"   # = declared.sha256 in the registry entry\n```\n\nThe lock test captures the surface from the source in memory (not from a build the publisher\nshipped), and the `server.json` test fails when the published block differs from the lock. A\nregistry hash that does not match the tag's lock means the entry was not published from that\nsource. Measured on 2026-10-07 for `bcb-br-mcp` 1.16.2 from a clean clone: lock, `server.json` and\nregistry entry carry the same `ff0973f91573…`.\n\n### 6.2 The promise holds per response, not per connection\n\nStep 3 checks the surface once, on connect. A server can answer `tools/list` one way at the start\nof a session and another way later, and the MCP spec only says it SHOULD send\n`notifications/tools/list_changed` when that happens; a server that wants to switch quietly just\nwon't. So a host that checks should check every list response it acts on, not only the first:\n\n1. Assemble the complete list, following `nextCursor` to the last page (§1). A single page is not\n   the list.\n2. Put it in place of the same list in the surface captured on connect, keeping `initialize` and\n   the other lists as captured (or re-fetch them too).\n3. Normalise (§2) and hash (§3) the whole surface again, and compare with `declared.sha256`.\n\nThe published hash covers the whole surface, never a list on its own, so a list response is not\nhashed by itself: it is checked by recomputing the surface with it in place. This needs no new\nfield and no new form. Treat a mismatch found mid-session exactly like one found on connect\n(step 5). The same applies to `resources/list`, `resources/templates/list` and `prompts/list`.\n\nRaised by a reader of the article that introduced this form (dev.to, comment 3gpa6, 2026-10-08).\n`exemplos/verify.mjs` is a one-shot check run outside any session, so it does not do this; it is\nwhat a host does inside one.\n\n## 7. Test vectors\n\n### 7.1 The basic vector\n\nThis raw capture:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"initialize\": {\n    \"protocolVersion\": \"2025-06-18\",\n    \"capabilities\": { \"tools\": { \"listChanged\": true } },\n    \"serverInfo\": { \"name\": \"exemplo\", \"version\": \"9.9.9\" }\n  },\n  \"tools\": [\n    { \"name\": \"b_tool\", \"inputSchema\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"required\": [\"z\", \"a\"] } },\n    { \"name\": \"B_tool\", \"inputSchema\": { \"type\": \"object\" } },\n    { \"name\": \"a_tool\", \"description\": \"Ç\", \"inputSchema\": { \"type\": \"object\" } }\n  ],\n  \"resources\": [],\n  \"resourceTemplates\": null,\n  \"prompts\": null\n}\n```\n\n(`resources` served and empty; templates and prompts not served.) Its canonical form serialises\nto:\n\n```\n{\"initialize\":{\"capabilities\":{\"tools\":{\"listChanged\":true}},\"instructions\":null,\"protocolVersion\":\"2025-06-18\",\"serverInfo\":{\"name\":\"exemplo\"}},\"prompts\":null,\"resourceTemplates\":null,\"resources\":[],\"tools\":[{\"inputSchema\":{\"type\":\"object\"},\"name\":\"B_tool\"},{\"description\":\"Ç\",\"inputSchema\":{\"type\":\"object\"},\"name\":\"a_tool\"},{\"inputSchema\":{\"required\":[\"z\",\"a\"],\"type\":\"object\"},\"name\":\"b_tool\"}]}\n```\n\nand its sha256 is `06c6e65c1e1a1e00d7659498e41bb0cd2fe76fe5248f3a92d2d05ddfc01351d8`.\n\nWhat the vector exercises: `tools` in code-unit order (`B` 0x42 < `a` 0x61 < `b` 0x62; a\nlocale-aware sort puts `B_tool` after `a_tool` and gives another hash); `required` keeping the\nserver's order (`z` before `a`); the `version` gone from `serverInfo`; `instructions` absent\nbecoming `null`; a non-ASCII character hashed as UTF-8; `[]` and `null` kept apart.\n\n### 7.2 Integer-like keys\n\nThis raw capture:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"initialize\": {\n    \"protocolVersion\": \"2025-06-18\",\n    \"capabilities\": {},\n    \"serverInfo\": { \"name\": \"exemplo\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\" }\n  },\n  \"tools\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"por_ano\",\n      \"inputSchema\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": { \"10\": { \"type\": \"string\" }, \"9\": { \"type\": \"string\" }, \"a\": { \"type\": \"string\" } }\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"resources\": null,\n  \"resourceTemplates\": null,\n  \"prompts\": null\n}\n```\n\nserialises to:\n\n```\n{\"initialize\":{\"capabilities\":{},\"instructions\":null,\"protocolVersion\":\"2025-06-18\",\"serverInfo\":{\"name\":\"exemplo\"}},\"prompts\":null,\"resourceTemplates\":null,\"resources\":null,\"tools\":[{\"inputSchema\":{\"properties\":{\"10\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"9\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"a\":{\"type\":\"string\"}},\"type\":\"object\"},\"name\":\"por_ano\"}]}\n```\n\nand its sha256 is `c7255a44a9d70ea6417ae2757c0530895b628551c3ae9fbfc440878a375539b1`. An\nimplementation that writes `\"9\"` before `\"10\"` (§3) gets another hash.\n\n## 8. What it does not prove\n\n- It cannot tell a host that a declared tool is benign. It proves that what the host runs is\n  what the registry published for that version (the registry does not let a published version's\n  metadata change), and, with §6.1, that the hash is the public source's. Judging what the surface\n  does stays a human decision on re-approval.\n- Without §6.1 the hash is the publisher's word. Not done, recorded as an idea: a signed\n  attestation (Sigstore, through the CI's OIDC identity, as npm provenance does) binding the hash\n  to the commit and the workflow, so a host could check it without rebuilding. It waits for a\n  host that verifies such attestations.\n- It covers the surface, not behaviour: tool results, resource contents and upstream data are\n  outside it.\n- It says nothing about versions published before the fingerprint was.\n\n## 9. Changes to this form\n\nAny change that can alter a sha256 for the same server gets a new form identifier\n(`mcp-surface/2`) and a CHANGELOG entry; the old identifier keeps its meaning. Changes that\ncannot alter a hash (wording, examples) keep the identifier.\n","numLines":286,"startLine":1,"totalLines":286}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Nw3dxJYsQvk46VfNPJPGd5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
